  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret the coefficient in a scaled impulse as the impulse area that weights $\\delta$(t) in continuous-time signal expressions.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Scaled Impulses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A scaled impulse is written as $A\\delta(t-a)$. Read it as one impulse located at $t=a$, with coefficient $A$ carrying the signed area, not the height or width.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t-a)\\,dt=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That unit-area rule is the baseline from the prior topic. Once the impulse is shifted to $t=a$, the shift changes location only; the coefficient still controls the area weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}A\\delta(t-a)\\,dt=A",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $A\\delta(t-a)$ has the same zero-width location behavior as $\\delta(t-a)$, but its signed area is $A$. Positive $A$ gives positive area, negative $A$ gives negative area, and fractional $A$ scales the same way.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=A\\delta(t-a)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $\\delta(t)$ as the base case, then read $A\\delta(t)$ and $A\\delta(t-a)$ with the same rule: the coefficient sets the impulse area, and the argument shift sets where that area sits. That separation is the idea to carry into the next examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Area of a Scaled Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Identify the area of each impulse in $2\\delta(t)$, $-\\delta(t-3)$, and $\\frac{1}{4}\\delta(t+2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient immediately in front of $\\delta(\\cdot)$. For a scaled impulse, that coefficient is the impulse area; the shift inside the argument only sets location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}2\\delta(t)\\,dt &= 2 \\\\\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}-\\delta(t-3)\\,dt &= -1 \\\\\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{4}\\delta(t+2)\\,dt &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read each coefficient exactly as it appears. The locations are $t=0$, $t=3$, and $t=-2$, but the areas are $2$, $-1$, and $\\frac{1}{4}$ because the sign and magnitude live in the coefficient, not in the shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{areas: } 2,\\ -1,\\ \\frac{1}{4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing a Scaled Impulse from a Given Area",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write expressions for area $3$ at $t=2$ and area $-\\frac{1}{2}$ at $t=-1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the requested area and the requested location. To write a scaled impulse, put the signed area in front and write the location as $t-a$ inside the $\\delta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n3\\delta(t-2) \\\\\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\delta(t+1)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So area $3$ at $t=2$ becomes $3\\delta(t-2)$, and area $-\\frac{1}{2}$ at $t=-1$ becomes $-\\frac{1}{2}\\delta(t+1)$. The shift chooses where the impulse sits; the coefficient gives the area and its sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Scaled Impulses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Negative and Fractional Impulse Weights",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=\\delta(t)-\\frac{3}{4}\\delta(t-1)+2\\delta(t+2)$, identify the signed area of each term and report the net area.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient in front of each $\\delta(\\cdot)$. Read that coefficient as the impulse area, and ignore the shift when you determine the area itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\delta(t) &\\rightarrow 1 \\\\\n-\\frac{3}{4}\\delta(t-1) &\\rightarrow -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n2\\delta(t+2) &\\rightarrow 2\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signed areas are $1$, $-\\frac{3}{4}$, and $2$. The locations are $t=0$, $t=1$, and $t=-2$, but the sign and size come from the coefficients, not from the shifts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n1-\\frac{3}{4}+2 &= \\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the prompt asks for the net area, add the coefficients after reading each term separately. The result is $\\frac{9}{4}$, which keeps the negative sign on the middle term and the fractional weight on the first term exactly as written.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
